WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A Toronto Star Profile this week framed Scott Shawyer's Vendée Globe attempt against the losses of Jerry Roof's and Derek Hatfield. Welcome to the very first episode of Offshore North. Today is August 18th, 2026. Every four years a small number of people sail alone, without stopping and without assistance all the way around the world. The race is called the Vendée Globe, it is the hardest thing in sailing, the kind of contest that gets called the Everest of the Seas, and no Canadian has ever finished.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.220
Scott Shawyer and Canada Ocean Racing are trying to change that at the edition that starts on November 12th, 2028. This show follows that attempt week by week and uses it as a door into the whole sport. Canadians have history here. A Canadian won the very first Route du Rhum in 1978, beating a much larger boat by 98 seconds after 23 days at sea. This series is the next chapter of that story. The sport is reported almost entirely in French.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:24.540
Stories reach English speakers late and stripped of meaning. What I want to do every Monday is close that gap. Each week the program covers the Canadian campaign segment with consequence rather than recap. The fleet round up with the week in Offshore Racing and the layer that explains why any of it matters. The weekly explainer with one concept explained properly and the countdown with the days left to the start and where qualification stands.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:49.780
Sources are named and linked every week. There is no hype, no play by play that will be stale by Monday, and nothing stated that the sources do not support. Uncertainty is flagged as uncertainty. The audience is three rings at once. Lifelong offshore fans who want the depth, the English press does not give them. Weekend sailors who know a boom from a bow, but not the points table, and people who have never touched a rope and never will.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:02:19.300
No sailing knowledge is required and none is assumed. Whatever needs explaining gets one clean sentence. I am an airline captain on the Boeing 737 and a lifelong recreational sailor. I cross the North Atlantic regularly through the summer, the same ocean and the same weather systems that every transatlantic race sails across. I am not an offshore racing expert and I say so plainly. What I bring is a working life spent reading weather, trading a shorter track against a faster one.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:49.580
And making irreversible calls on incomplete information at three in the morning. Plus having felt at a far smaller scale, what it is like when a hole comes unstuck and starts to fly. Offshore North is part of the Nerra network. It is an independent ad-free network of daily shows built by two friends. Novak plus Perra equals Nerra. The shows cover technology, science, markets, world news, and language learning in several languages. They are free with no ads and no pay while at NerraNetwork.com.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:03:18.240
On the Canadian boat, as of the team's August 17, 2026 update, Emyra 4 had departed Collingwood the weekend of eight to nine August for the return transatlantic delivery to France. Canada Ocean Racing's own site posted a Toronto star interview with Scott Shire on 17 August. The piece places Shire's 2028 Vendée Globe goal against the 1997 disappearance of Jerry Roof's and the later death of Derek Hatfield.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:32.280
Roof's was lying second in the 1996 to 1997 Vendée Globe when his last message described 15 meter seas and winds above 80 knots. He was never heard from again and wreckage reached the Chilean coast months later.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:40.260
Hatfield campaigned spear of Canada through the round the world races of the 2000s and started a Vendée Globe attempt that fell short.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:04:01.240
Shire, who came to the sport from 26 years as president and CEO of JMP Solutions, completed his first solo Imoka race in the 2024 New York Vendée aboard the 2011 Owen Clark design, now named Bewater Positive, before acquiring the current generation foiling Imoka Emyra 4 in 2025.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:11.040
The Toronto star framing treats these earlier Canadian losses as the direct backdrop for Shire's stated intention to become the first to finish, now the fleet.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:24.880
Philly parts after 17 years in the French Navy is preparing an Imoka campaign for the 2028 Vendée Globe. The move adds another experienced solo sailor to the growing list of entrants already working on qualification.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:35.940
Hearts left the Navy to pursue the race full time and had spoken publicly about the transition from military service to the demands of solo ocean racing on a 60 foot foiling monohol.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:51.620
His entry increases the number of skippers with documented offshore and navigation experience targeting the same 2028 start line that Shire must reach through the published qualification path of at least one grade two solo race finished within twice the winner's time.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:05:04.340
The 1997 Vendée Globe winner Imoka Geodes will start the 2026 Root-do-Rum. The boats return to the start line keeps a proven hull in the fleet while newer designs continue to launch.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:16.840
Geodes sailed by Kristoff Aguin to victory in the 1996 to 1997 edition will race again under its original name in the solo transatlantic event that begins first November from Saint Molo.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:27.500
His participation demonstrates that older race proven Imokas remain competitive options even as teams such as Sebastian Simon's launch new builds like Group Dupre II.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:38.520
A Skull-butt column examined whether modern Imoka cockpits which allow the boat to be sailed largely from below conflict with the CoLRGU requirement for a proper lookout by site.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.520
The question directly affects how the class will be raced and regulated in future solo events.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:55.220
The column notes that Imoka 60s are now designed to be fully sailed indoors and that CoLRGU rule 5 requires every vessel to maintain a proper lookout by site at all times.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:05.900
Faster boats mean less time on deck raising the practical tension between design choices that reduce physical exposure and the international collision regulations that predate foiling monoholes.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:13.120
Time for plain sailing. The Vendée Globe is the only round the world race that is solo, non-stop and unassisted.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:24.880
A Skipper leaves Lesabeladolone, sails south through the Atlantic, rounds the three great capes of the southern ocean, then returns the same way without any outside help and without ever stopping the boat.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:33.760
The 2024 to 25 edition was won by Charlie Dalen in 64 days 19 hours, 9 days inside the previous record.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:44.160
Because the race is unassisted, every repair, every routing decision and every meal must be handled by one person on a 60 foot foiling monohole that can exceed 40 knots.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:53.660
The boat carries roughly 5000 calories a day in freeze dried food, makes its own water from the sea and generates electricity from hydro generators and solar panels.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:07:00.020
Sleep comes in fragments of 20 minutes while the autopilot steers. No Canadian has ever finished the race.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:08.320
That single fact is why the campaign exists and why every qualifying race shires sales on emura for now carries an extra layer.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:19.040
Each completed Grade 2 solo event, though with banks points toward the 2028 entry list and proves the boat and Skipper can survive the conditions that have already claimed lives in the fleets history.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:26.480
The published qualification outline requires at least one such Grade 2 solo finish on the boat that will be raced in the Vendée Globe.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:34.520
Completed within twice the winners time, plus a top 37 ranking in the Emoka Globe Series Championship ahead of the 40 boat cap.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:42.220
Shires first opportunity at that requirement arrives with the route do room on November 1st, 2026, and the countdown.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.820
812 days remain until the Vendée Globe start on November 12th, 2028.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:55.260
The campaign's next concrete milestone is the route do room, which starts on November 1st, 2026 from Saint Malau.

